  "preamble": "Near-close [ʊ] is tensed and raised to [u], usually by merging with an older /u/. Swahili and Zulu merged Proto-Bantu *ʊ with *u (Proto-Bantu *-tátʊ̀ 'three' > Swahili -tatu, Zulu -thathu). In Sardinian and Romanian Latin short ŭ, pronounced [ʊ], merged with ū as /u/ (Latin furnus [ˈfʊr.nʊs] > Sardinian furru, bucca [ˈbʊk.ka] > Romanian bucă), whereas Italian and Spanish lowered it to o.",
